# QoS = Quality of Service.

In networking and distributed systems it means: the delivery guarantees and performance expectations a service provides for messages.

In practice, for messaging systems like the one we’re sketching:

Delivery guarantees:

At-most-once (fire and forget; may be dropped).

At-least-once (will be retried until acknowledged; possible duplicates).

Exactly-once (delivered once and only once; expensive).

Ordering: whether messages are guaranteed to arrive in the order sent.

Durability: whether messages survive crashes/restarts.

Latency/Bandwidth constraints: whether certain messages are prioritized (e.g., “story event” vs “chat spam”).

When our ADRs mention “QoS” in future growth: it means reserving envelope fields like seq, ttl, priority, so later we can introduce stronger guarantees (retry, ordering, durability, prioritization) without breaking the protocol.
